    "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios",
    "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})=$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
